// Generated by scripts/restructure-by-construct.
// Per-construct DSL layout: every <kind>s.memql file in a domain
// holds every construct of that kind in the domain. The companion
// files cover the other construct kinds.

// ---- logic/v1/cluster/bootstrapCluster.memql ----

// Bootstrap the cluster's self-representation in the database on first startup.
// Decides whether the cluster / database / identity-provider rows need
// creating; the calling automation owns the conditional writes. Only BFF
// nodes create. Idempotent: decides "create nothing" once a cluster exists.
use cluster.queries.{ existingCluster, staleClusterNodes }
use platform.queries.{ globalVariable }

// PURE decision logic (ADR S2.1 logic-purity, #2235). It performs the
// existingCluster() read + the bff-only / no-cluster gating and returns a
// two-field decision object; the three conditional create writes
// (createDatabase / createIdentityProvider / createCluster) moved onto the
// bootstrapCluster automation as `if`-gated mutation steps. No graph write
// survives in the logic, so the logic-purity baseline entry was removed in the
// same change.
//
// Returns a single boolean `create`: true only when NO cluster row exists yet
// AND this node is the bff. That captures BOTH idempotency guards (skip-if-
// exists + bff-only) the three original `if existing.empty() &&
// node.type=="bff"` guards shared. The automation ANDs this with an
// `exists(payload.identityProvider)` event check for the idp create alone, so
// the identityProvider row lands only on the bff create path AND when a
// provider is configured (the extra guard the idp create had).
@description("Decides whether to run the cluster bootstrap writes on first startup (ADR S2.1 pure logic, #2235). Reads existingCluster() and returns the boolean `create` -- true only when no cluster row exists yet AND this node is the bff (both idempotency guards). The calling automation gates the three conditional creates (v1:cluster:database / :identityProvider / :cluster) on `steps.decide.result == true`, with the idp create additionally requiring an identityProvider block in the startup envelope.")
logic bootstrapCluster {
  args {
    event object @required
  }
  body {
  existing := query existingCluster()
  return existing.empty() && args.event.payload.node.type == "bff"
  }
}

// ---- logic/v1/cluster/pruneStaleClusterNodes.memql ----

// pruneStaleClusterNodes
// ======================
//
// Heartbeat-based prune of departed cluster nodes (#1061). The
// v1:cluster:node store is append-only: NodeStatusWriter appends a row
// on every liveness transition, and across K8s rollouts (nodes keyed by
// pod name, #1042) rows for long-gone pods accumulate forever. This cron
// marks those rows terminal so topology / CLI / WorkerDialer stop
// treating dead pods as live.
//
// BACKSTOP to the fast reaper (#1874). The active reconciliation loop
// (TopologyReconciler, component/node/reconciler.go) is the primary path now
// -- it retires superseded-deployment orphans (supersededDeployments)
// immediately and mesh-absent nodes within a short grace window, in seconds.
// This 30-min prune stays only as the lazy safety net for whatever the fast
// path missed. Both append the SAME idempotent terminal health="stopped" and
// staleClusterNodes excludes already-stopped rows, so they can never
// write conflicting statuses for the same node.
//
// PURE decision logic (ADR S2.1 logic-purity, #2235). The logic resolves the
// stale window, computes the cutoff timestamp, and returns the LIST of rows to
// retire; the per-row terminal write (updateNodeHealth) moved onto the
// pruneStaleClusterNodes automation as a `forEach` step. No graph write
// survives in the logic, so the logic-purity baseline entry was removed in the
// same change.
//
// Mechanism:
//   1. The stale window is read from the global variable registry
//      (MEMQL_NODE_STALE_PRUNE_MINUTES), so only CONFIG differs per env
//      -- the code/architecture is identical local-cluster and staging.
//      Default 30 minutes via coalesce: comfortably longer than the
//      in-memory peer windows (offline=25s, stale-gossip=60s) so a node
//      that is merely mid-restart is not pruned, but short enough that a
//      genuinely-departed pod clears within an hour. Set
//      `make variable-set NAME=MEMQL_NODE_STALE_PRUNE_MINUTES VALUE=...
//      SCOPE=global` (or the process env) to override per environment.
//   2. The cutoff is `now - window`: addDuration with a NEGATIVE ISO-8601
//      duration ("PT-30M") subtracts the window from the current timestamp.
//      This replaces the old per-row `lastSeen + window < now` `if` guard --
//      the window math now lives ONCE here instead of per row, and the
//      comparison pushes down into the query (next step).
//   3. staleClusterNodes({ olderThan: cutoff }) pushes a
//      `payload.lastSeen < cutoff` predicate onto the query (#1642), so the
//      query returns ONLY the latest-non-stopped rows past the window --
//      already filtered, idempotent (asOf latest), and excluding rows already
//      health="stopped". The logic returns that node list for the automation
//      to retire.
//   4. Prune (on the automation) == write a terminal health="stopped" row
//      (append-only; we don't hard-delete). cluster:node has no
//      `deleted`/`active` field and no soft-delete trait, and consumers
//      already treat "stopped" as gone: WorkerDialer skips offline/stopped
//      rows, and the CLI / topology dedupe to latest-per-id so the stopped
//      marker hides the node from the live set.
//
// Runs every 10 minutes. The cluster node table is small (one row-stream
// per pod ever seen), so a frequent walk is cheap and keeps topology
// fresh shortly after a rollover.
@description("Decides which departed cluster nodes to retire (ADR S2.1 pure logic, #2235). Reads MEMQL_NODE_STALE_PRUNE_MINUTES (default 30), computes cutoff = now - window via addDuration with a negative ISO duration, and returns staleClusterNodes({olderThan: cutoff}).nodes() -- the LATEST non-stopped rows whose lastSeen is past the window (the olderThan arg pushes a payload.lastSeen<cutoff predicate onto the query, #1642). The calling automation appends the terminal health='stopped' row per returned node via a forEach updateNodeHealth step.")
logic pruneStaleClusterNodes {
  args {
    event object @required
  }
  body {
  windowMinutes := query globalVariable( name: "MEMQL_NODE_STALE_PRUNE_MINUTES" )
  stale := query staleClusterNodes( olderThan: addDuration(now, concat("PT-", coalesce(windowMinutes.first().payload.value, "30"), "M")) )
  return stale.nodes()
  }
}
